          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The
            %strong well-ordering theorem
            is a famous result in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set+theory"} set theory
            stating that every
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
            may be
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/well-order"} well-ordered
          %p
            Fundamental for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Georg+Cantor"} G. Cantor's
            approach to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinal+arithmetic"} ordinal arithmetic
            it was an open problem until
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ernst+Zermelo"} E. Zermelo
            gave a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proof"} proof
            in 1904 using the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+choice"} axiom of choice
            (to which it is in fact equivalent).
          %p
            Hence the well-ordering theorem is one of the many equivalent formulations of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+choice"} axiom of choice
            like also e.g.
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Zorn%27s+lemma"} Zorn's lemma
          %h2#statement_and_proof Statement and proof
          %p
            Given any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/well-order"} well-order
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ≺
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \prec
            on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
          %p
            The first proof was given in (
            %a{:href => "#Zermelo04"}> Zermelo 1904
            ). Within the nLab, the article
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Zorn%27s+lemma"} Zorn's lemma
            gives a standard informal proof that can be formalized in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ZFC"} ZFC
            under
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+logic"} classical logic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+choice"} axiom of choice
            and Zorn’s lemma.
          %p
          .num_remark
            %h6 Remark
            %p
              That the well-ordering theorem is more of a
              %em theorem
              in need of a proof, while the axiom of choice is more of an
              %em axiom
              to be assumed without proof is, of course, a matter of opinion, but it's reflected in Jerry Bona's famous quotation:
            %blockquote
              %p
                The Axiom of Choice is obviously true, the well-ordering principle obviously false, and who can tell about
                = succeed "?" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Zorn%27s+lemma"} Zorn's lemma
            %p
          %h2#consequences Consequences
          %p
            If every set can be well-ordered, then the natural map from
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinal+number"} ordinal number
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cardinal+number"} cardinal number
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/surjection"} surjection
            %em smallest
            ordinal number with that cardinality. (This does require
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/excluded+middle"} excluded middle
            %em defined
            to be an ordinal number that is
            = succeed ":" do
              %em initial
          %p
            As in the argument above, the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+choice"} axiom of choice
            follows; given any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/surjection"} surjection
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f: A \to B
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            and then split
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            by mapping an element
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            to the smallest element
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            such that
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi y
                    %mo =
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y = f(x)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/excluded+middle"} excluded middle
            to show that such a smallest element exists, so the well-ordering principle does not (seem to) imply the axiom of choice constructively.
          %p To get the large (or “global”) axiom of choice (that any surjection between proper classes splits), we need a large well-ordering theorem: that every proper class can be well-ordered. The large principles do not follow from the small ones.
          %h2#in_constructive_mathematics In constructive mathematics
          %p
            In
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+mathematics"} constructive mathematics
            %a{:href => "https://hott.zulipchat.com/#narrow/stream/228519-general/topic/inductive.20well-ordering.20gives.20excluded.20middle.3F/near/246863644"} proved by Andrew Swan
            (2021) and formalized in
            %a{:href => "https://www.cs.bham.ac.uk/~mhe/TypeTopology/WellOrderingTaboo.html"} Agda by Tom de Jong
            (2021) and in
            %a{:href => "https://github.com/dominik-kirst/sierpinski-hott/blob/master/coq/orders_lem.v"} Coq by Dominik Kirst
            (2021).
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Zorn%27s+lemma"} Zorn's lemma
            is, on the other hand, constructively weaker than the axiom of choice, as it doesn’t even imply excluded middle. But together with excluded middle it implies choice. However, Zorn’s lemma is not particularly useful without excluded middle.
          %h2#related_entries Related entries
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Zorn%27s+lemma"} Zorn's lemma
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+choice"} axiom of choice
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hartogs+number"} Hartogs number
          %h2#history History
          %p
            %strong Georg Cantor
            first developed
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set+theory"} set theory
            in the context of studying well-ordered sets of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+number"} real number
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinal+numbers"} ordinal numbers
            %em ‘fundamental and weighty law of thought that is remarkable for his generality’
            and promised to come back to it later (
            %a{:href => "#Cantor32"}> Cantor 1932
            , p.169). In the following, he announced proofs but they failed to materialize so that he was forced to take it as an assumption.
            %sup#fnref:1
              %a{:href => "#fn:1", :rel => "footnote"} 1
          %p
            Consequently, the well-ordering principle ended as ‘a very strange claim’ second on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilbert%27s+problems"} Hilbert's millennium list
            of open problems in mathematics in 1900. Then in 1904, the Hungarian mathematician J. König announced a proof that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuum"} continuum
            could not be well-ordered but had to retract the proof.
          %p
            Soon afterwards in 1904,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ernst+Zermelo"} Ernst Zermelo
            finally gave a proof using the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+choice"} axiom of choice
            following a suggestion by E. Schmidt. The proof, albeit correct, was met with heavy criticism by prominent mathematicians so that Zermelo published a new proof and a defense of the contested axiom of choice in 1908.
          %p
            The attempt to make explicit the set-theoretic assumptions in the proof led him to publish his axioms for set theory in the same year which became later a part of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Zermelo-Fraenkel+set+theory"} Zermelo-Fraenkel set theory
            = succeed ":" do
              %em decisive watershed for the development of modern mathematics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/foundation+of+mathematics"} foundation of mathematics
            and putting the problem of non-constructive methods of proof on the agenda.
          %h2#references References
          %p The original proof is in
          %ul#Zermelo04
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ernst+Zermelo"}> Ernst Zermelo
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Beweis, daß jede Menge wohlgeordnet werden kann
              %strong 59
              (1904) pp.514-516. (
              %a{:href => "http://gdz.sub.uni-goettingen.de/no_cache/en/dms/load/img/?IDDOC=28526"}> gdz
              )
          %p The second proof together with an eloquent defense of the axiom of choice can be found in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ernst+Zermelo"}> Ernst Zermelo
              ,
              %em Neuer Beweis für die Möglichkeit einer Wohlordnung
              , Mathematische Annalen
              %strong 65
              (1908) pp.107-128. (
              %a{:href => "http://gdz.sub.uni-goettingen.de/dms/load/img/?PPN=GDZPPN002261952"}> gdz
              )
          %p Cantor’s texts are collected together with comments by Zermelo in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ernst+Zermelo"} Ernst Zermelo
              (ed.),
              %em Georg Cantor - Gesammelte Abhandlungen Mathematischen und Philosophischen Inhalts
              , Springer Berlin 1932. (
              %a{:href => "http://gdz.sub.uni-goettingen.de/dms/load/toc/?PPN=PPN237853094"}> gdz
              )
          %p English versions of Zermelo’s papers are in
          %ul
            %li
              J. van Heijenoort (ed.),
              %em From Frege to Gödel - A Source Book in Mathematical Logic 1879-1931
              , Harvard UP 1967.
          %p On the relation between AC and the well-ordering principle in general toposes see
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+Freyd"}> Peter Freyd
                ,
                %em Choice and Well-ordering
                , APAL
                %strong 35
                (1987) pp.149-166.
            %li
              %p
                M.-M. Mawanda,
                %em Well-ordering and Choice in Toposes
                , JPAA
                %strong 50
                (1988) pp.171-184.
            %li#Johnstone
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+Johnstone"}> Peter Johnstone
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sketches+of+an+Elephant"} Sketches of an Elephant
                    vol. II
            %li
              %p
                J. Todd Wilson, “An Intuitionistic version of Zermelo’s proof that every choice set can be well-ordered”, J. Symbolic Logic, 66:3 (2001), 1121–1126; (
                %a{:href => "http://www.jstor.org/stable/2695096"}> JSTOR
                \: paywalled),
                = succeed "." do
                  %a{:href => "https://github.com/lambdacalculator/lean-choice"} formalization in the Lean Theorem Prover
          %p The proof that Zorn’s Lemma doesn’t imply excluded middle (and hence doesn’t imply choice without assuming excluded middle):
          %ul
            %li#Bell
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Lane+Bell"}> John Lane Bell
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Zorn’s lemma and complete Boolean algebras in intuitionistic type theories
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.2307/2275642"} doi:10.2307/2275642
          .footnotes
            %hr/
            %ol
              %li#fn:1
                %p
                  This contrasts with Cantor’s attitude towards the axiom of choice which he used implicitly but never thematised explicitly. In fact, the full explicit awareness of the use of the axiom choice in mathematics had to await the controversy over Zermelo’s well-ordering theorem in 1904 (with some anticipation by G. Peano and B. Levi earlier).
                  %a{:href => "#fnref:1", :rev => "footnote"} ↩
